Migrating Forest — Core Mechanics Lockdown
Migrating Forest — Core Mechanics Lockdown

This document establishes hard mechanical truths about the Migrating Forest, specifically for the entrance band where the party first crosses.

These rules are not flavor. They are constraints you should rely on when adjudicating movement, pressure, and player plans.
Geographic Constants (Entrance Band)

    Forest band width: ~40 km (entire migrating mass)

    Entrance thinning: ~0.5 km wide corridor where the forest is least dense

    Canopy height (this region): ~30 meters

    Typical trunk diameter: Up to ~10 meters for mature trees

This entrance exists not because the forest is weak here, but because movement pressure is lowest at this edge.

It is the safest crossing point available.
Travel Rate

    Effective movement: ~1 km per day while inside the forest

This rate assumes:

    Careful navigation

    Periodic detours

    Minor forest correction

Attempting to exceed this rate:

    Increases Forest Attention

    Accelerates Dinosaur Aggression

    Risks partial backtracking as paths subtly re-route

Travel is never blocked outright, but speed is always taxed.
Vertical Space

    Combat, movement, and navigation all occur in three dimensions

    Ground, mid-canopy, and upper-canopy are all accessible

Key implications:

    Vertical movement often avoids attention

    Horizontal permanence draws correction

    The forest tolerates light, temporary vertical presence better than ground disruption

The Branchbound exploit this aggressively.
Forest Aversion Substances

The forest actively dislikes and will redirect away from the following:

    Salt

    Oil

    Fire

    Intense cold

How Aversion Manifests

Aversion does not cause immediate hostility. Instead, the forest responds by:

    Redirecting root growth

    Opening alternative paths away from the substance

    Increasing density elsewhere

Used sparingly, these substances:

    Can create temporary corridors, buffers, or delays

    Can influence migration direction subtly

Used excessively or repeatedly:

    Accelerates Storm Escalation

    Attracts storm-scarred predators

    Marks the party as disruptive rather than adaptive

What the Forest Tolerates

The forest is largely indifferent to:

    Temporary structures

    Rope, lashings, and light platforms

    Quiet passage

    Abandonment without cleanup

It reacts poorly to:

    Straight lines

    Long-term camps

    Repeated fire use

    Attempts to "hold" territory

Entrance-Specific Play Guidance

This thinning is where players learn the rules.

In this zone:

    The forest corrects gently

    Dinosaurs are present but cautious

    Branchbound influence is indirect

Use this space to teach:

    Speed has a cost

    Verticality matters

    Aversion substances are tools, not weapons

If players understand this zone, they will survive deeper crossings.
Design Mandate

When adjudicating edge cases, ask:

    Is this action light or permanent?

    Does it help the forest move, or resist it?

The forest responds accordingly.

