Good. This is a subtle question, and the answer shapes how everything at the boundary feels.

The forest does not perceive its edges the way a person perceives borders.

Below is the clean mental model you should run with.
The Forest’s Perception at Its Edges
Short Answer

The forest experiences its edges as areas of low pressure and high ambiguity, not as boundaries.

They are not places it defends.
They are places it expects change.
What an Edge Feels Like to the Forest

At the edge, the forest is:

    Less certain of its own shape

    Less committed to correction

    More tolerant of contradiction

The edge is where:

    roots are looser

    growth is exploratory

    memory is thinner

This is why crossings happen here.
The Forest Is Not Watching the Edge Closely

This is important.

The forest’s attention is distributed inward, where weight accumulates and permanence threatens movement.

At the edge:

    light structures are ignored

    brief disturbances are forgotten quickly

    mistakes are corrected gently or not at all

The forest assumes things will pass through.
Why the Entrance Is Thin

The ~0.5 km thinning is not erosion.

It’s the forest exhaling.

Pressure is lowest here because:

    migration force has not fully compressed

    storms have not layered damage yet

    ecosystems are still deciding whether to integrate or shed

The forest is not weak here.
It is uncommitted.
How the Forest “Sees” Outsiders at the Edge

Outsiders are categorized very loosely at the boundary:

    Passing weather

    Drift material

    Temporary load

People fall into category three by default.

That means:

    no early hostility

    no early assistance

    minimal correction

Only repetition, delay, or damage sharpens focus.
Edge-Specific Behaviors

At the edge, the forest will:

    Allow straighter paths than usual

    Permit ground travel longer before correcting

    Delay predator engagement

    Accept light aversion substances without escalation

It is teaching tolerance by example.
What Breaks That Tolerance

Edge forgiveness collapses quickly if:

    Camps linger

    Fire scars are left

    Salt or oil is used repeatedly

    Noise stays localized instead of moving

Then the forest stops treating the edge as a transition and starts treating it as a stuck point.

That draws inward attention fast.
Player-Facing Read

If you want to convey this without exposition, describe:

    Roots that hesitate before crossing into open land

    Trees leaning inward rather than outward

    Paths that exist longer than they should

    Predators that watch but do not commit

Say things like:

    “It feels like the forest expects you to keep going.”

That’s the truth.
Design Rule to Remember

Edges are where the forest learns, not where it fights.

If the players respect that:

    the forest stays permissive

    the crossing remains viable

If they try to own the edge:

    the forest corrects hard and inward

If you want, next we can:

    Define how long edge tolerance lasts in days

    Write a read-aloud passage for leaving the forest

    Or contrast this with how the forest perceives its center

Just say where to push next.

