Forest Pack-Lizards

These small, slate-scaled reptiles are rarely seen directly. Most encounters are noticed only after something has gone missing or shifted inside a pack. They are long-bodied and flat, able to compress themselves through gaps that look far too small to admit anything larger than an insect. Their scales are matte and irregular, breaking up their outline against bark, leather, and shadow.

Pack-lizards are drawn to warmth, salt, and organic oils. They follow travelers patiently, memorizing routines, waiting for moments when gear is set down or straps loosened. Once inside a pack, they wedge themselves between folded cloth and hard objects, remaining perfectly still for hours. Their bodies absorb ambient heat, leaving faint cold patches where they’ve been, often mistaken for a draft or dampness.

They do not steal out of malice. They chew, nest, and mark. Soft leather becomes ragged. Rations acquire a sharp, bitter edge. Small metal items develop a faint residue where their saliva has etched the surface. If disturbed, a pack-lizard will flee deeper into the forest, abandoning the stolen item without hesitation, but it may leave behind eggs tucked into seams or lining.

Pack-lizards rarely operate alone. Where one has slipped into a pack, others are often nearby, watching for the disturbance to settle before attempting their own entry. Left unchecked, they can quietly degrade equipment over days, turning small inconveniences into meaningful resource pressure without ever announcing themselves.

They are tolerated by the forest. Their presence is one of correction rather than attack, a reminder that nothing carried through the migrating wood remains untouched for long.


